  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nOur approach combines several innovative techniques to tackle the Erdos minimum overlap problem. Unlike the previous solution which relied on temperature annealing and local optimization, we employ a multi-resolution spectral relaxation approach combined with a physics-inspired optimization. The key idea is to represent the step function as a superposition of basis functions and use continuous optimization over these representations. This differs significantly from grid-based methods as it leverages mathematical transforms and gradient information more effectively.\n\nThe approach uses a sequence of transformations including discrete cosine transforms (DCT) and wavelet transforms to project the problem into different domains. This allows us to:\n1. Reduce the effective dimensionality with compressed representations\n2. Enforce monotonicity constraints more naturally\n3. Capture periodic structures implicitly\n4. Handle the non-convex nature with penalty methods\n\nThe algorithm alternates between constrained optimization in k-space (frequency domain) and direct nonlinear optimization, with a specialized curvature estimator that maintains feasible solutions. We also incorporate a restart mechanism based on adaptive basis selection, switching between localized and global optimization modes.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n- Input: Noval representation vector $s \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$ parametrizing a step function on [0,2]\n- Objective:\n  $$\\min_s \\max_k \\iint_{|x-y|=k} h(x)(1-h(y))dxdy$$\n  (handled via spectral projection)\n- Constraints: \n  $\\sum w_i s_i = \\frac{n}{2}$, $|s_i|\\leq 1$ (handled via logarithmic barriers)\n- Projection method: DCT-VIII transform \n  $$T = \\frac{4}{n} \\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} w_k s_k \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi k m}{n}\\right)$$\n  for suitable weights $w_k$\n\nWe initialize with orthogonal basis functions (discrete cosine functions) and allow selective refinement through:\n1. Canonical duality transformation $(s,t) \\mapsfrom s + t\\Delta$\n2. Gradient-based updates $\\nabla_s \\mathcal{L} = g + \\mu \\cdot \\nabla_s \\phi(s,t)$\n3. Penalty parameter evolution $\\mu \\leftarrow \\mu \\tau$\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Parent initialization:\n   - Validate initial_h_values (shape (n,))\n   - Compute min distance to boundaries\n   - Transform to frequency space version through orthogonal projection\n   - Apply logarithmic scaling $s_i = \\log(1/h_i)$ to handle bounds\n\n2. Primary optimization loop (with budget management):\n   - Stage 1 (10% budget): Full-grid spectral analysis with DFSAN-CMA\n   - Stage 2 (50% budget): Multi-basis optimization with adaptive restarts\n   - Stage 3 (40% budget): Refinement phase with hierarchical sampling\n\n3. Core optimization steps:\n   - Transform step: $T = W \\cdot s + \\mu$\n   - Monotonicity projection: $P_\\delta = \\exp(-(\\delta/l)^2)$\n   - Duality gap reduction:Use ellipsoid method through penalty scaling\n   - Constraint satisfaction via Nuclear Norm penalty\n\n4. Restart mechanism:\n   - Implements three types of restarts:\n     1. Geometric restart $s_{new} = \\alpha s + \\beta (1-s)$\n     2. Spectral restart $s_{new} = T(s) \\cdot T^{-1}(initial)$\n     3. Canonical dual restart $s_{new} = \\max\\{0, \\min\\{1, s + t\\}$\n\n5. Validation and fallback:\n   - Ensure $\\sum h_i \\cdot w_i = n/2$\n   - Check non-negativity with tangent space projection\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n1. Basis selection:\n   - Type A: Canonical basis (winning variation)\n   - Type B: Discrete Fourier basis (conditional)\n   - Type C: Wavelet packet basis (fallback)\n\n2. Adaptive strategies:\n   - Grid refinement:\u6bcf\u5f53\u8bef\u5dee\u8d85\u8fc7\u9608\u503c\u65f6\uff0c\u7f51\u683c\u4e0a\u91c7\u6837\n   - Damping parameter: $\\alpha \\in [0.95, 0.999]$ adaptive\n   - Restart frequency: \u6bcf50\u6b21\u8fed\u4ee3\u4e00\u6b21\n\n3. Dimensionality:\n   - JobControl dimension: $d$=40 minimum\u7ef4\u5ea6\n   - Worker dimension: $w$ based on previous success\n\nCompute Budget and Robustness:\n- Primary task: ~30 seconds  \n- Full optimization: 80 seconds\n- Total: <1100 seconds budget\n\nReturns the best found s vector (non-negative with sum n/2) and transformed version ensuring both constraints.\n\nThe solution allows adaptive representation switching and maintains gradient information through proper barrier function construction.\n\nFor implementation details:\n- Use $\\mu = 10^5$ logarithmic barrier parameter\n- Set $T_{\\min}=0.5^\\circ K$ minimum temperature\n- Decision variable acceptance with: $\\Delta f \\cdot \\exp(-\\Delta f / T_{\\text{cook}}$\n- Strictly adhere to: $||\\nabla^2 f||_2 < \\epsilon$ stopping condition\n\nThe design ensures we escape local optima through basis switches while maintaining mathematical form. Unlike the parent solution, we focus on transform domain manipulation and provide complete gradient recovery.",
